A||||5||ChadMan||FIEROLESS!  (88 brake woes)||||8||
Z||000000||ChadMan||05-28-2000||09:28 AM||||So I had this bright idea that I'd put new brake pads on my 88 Coupe yesterday.  All four rotors are nice and smooth with no discoloration or cracks.  I figured the job would take less than two hours.<P>WRONG - I discovered that the left front caliper had torn boot on one of the little sleeve/slider deals (technical term) that allows the caliper to slide back and forth.  The right front caliper had a stuck piston (guess I know why my car pulled to the left under heavy braking.)<P>I called NAPA, Advance Auto and AutoZone, and not one store in greater Columbus, OH has calipers for my car!  And because of the holiday, nobody can order them until Tuesday!  What's more, Advance charges extra for the sliders/sleeves and NAPA doesn't even carry them.<P>I wound up ordering my calipers from the Fiero Store - $80/side but they said they come with the sliders.  But they probably won't be here until Thursday.<P>I love my 88's brakes.  But if I had an 84-87, I could have gotten my calipers here in town for half the cost and been driving my Fiero all week instead of my mother's '78 Caprice.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif"><P>Thanks for letting me rant.  Oh well - I thought the brakes were pretty good before - I can't wait to see how good my new brakes will be!||||reg||8
Z||000001||Fformula88||05-28-2000||10:14 AM||||I feel your pain Chadman.  I had to replace my rear pads this spring.  It took me forever to get them replaced.  First it was a figth with the springs and those rods that hold them in.  To pund them out, I had to get a tap in through the parking brake equipment.  I got them out, and couldn't get the pistons in.  On the rears, the must be turned in like a screw because of the paerking brake.  I ended up having to remove the bridge to do this, and it took a LOT of effort.  Needless to say after working for hours on these things, I finally got them back together.  Then they both LEAKED.  So I had to tear them apart again.  After discovering the price of 88 Calipers, I ended up rebuilding them.  I ordered the kits from Fiero Store since Napa said it would take 5 days to special order the kits.  They work great now that they are done.  It was very nice of GM to redesign the brakes and try to make them better... but why couldn't they put them on some other GM cars?  It would have been nice to make more of them so they wouldn't be so expensive.  Oh, and a local repair shop quoated me $800 for the job, thats why I did them myself!||||reg||1
Z||000002||Sage||05-28-2000||12:32 PM||||Chad, sorry to hear you are without your coupe temporarily!  Just did the brakes on my sonw 88 after putting it back together and ran into the same thing.  The drivers side front had frozen sleeves.  You can run it without the boots for a while, especially if you are going to replace the caliper anyway.  It took about an hour to free up the sliders but it put the car back on the road.  Could have gotten by with just new boots, but you can't get them.  A slider kit was $46.00 @ Pontiac, and a caliper(reman)was $43.00 @ Advance.  When you get the caliper, they give you just the"bridge" the actual caliper,(new piston, seals,sliders,boots) you have to separate your old one and take back the core($80.00core charge!)then you just bolt the new one to the old housing, put in the pads and brand new brakes!  Just thought I would mention this in case you didn't want to wait. It means taking it apart twice, but it also gets you your car back. Good luck!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000003||SCCA FIERO||05-28-2000||05:16 PM||||Chadman, you may be a little steamed right now, but getting reman calipers was a good idea. They will be god for another 10 years. And when all your frustration wears off and you take it for a spin the grin will be stuck there for a week.||||reg||7
Z||000004||ChadMan||05-29-2000||09:26 AM||||Thanks for the sympathy guys!<P>Hey Fformula88, when you did your rear brakes, do you mean that you had to turn the actual piston in order to seat it?  And if so, did you turn it clockwise to seat it like any other threaded fastener?<P>I'm doing the rears too and I hope to be able to install new pads on the old calipers.  But I probably won't get that lucky.<P>Thanks!||||reg||1
Z||000005||Fformula88||05-29-2000||01:06 PM||||Chadman... Yep, you have to turn the actual piston to seat it... if it goes in.  I had to put a lot of downforce on it too, which meant taking the bridge off.  My Haynes manual says that you can use needlenose plyers, but I bought a socket wrench attachment at Napa for about $8.  It was much easier to use.  <P>Also, each side turns a different direction to seat it.  One is clockwise and the other is counterclockwise.  I don't remember which one is which, but if you start to turn it the wrong way it will come out more.  So its easy to figure out which way to turn them.  I have heard from others that they are supposed to turn in easy, but mine were still very difficult to turn in after I rebuilt them.  Don't be afraid to apply pressure.  Good luck!||||reg||1
